Word of the Week: 24/7
A relationship in which power-exchange protocols are in place all the time, not just during scenes. The dynamic carries into daily life, from morning routines to how decisions get made.


💬 Join the conversation: Have you tried a 24/7 dynamic, or would you want to? How do you balance protocol with real life: work, kids, bills and bad days? How would you define 24/7?
=====
Word of the Week: Acomoclitism
A preference or need for oneself or one’s partner to have hairless genitals. It can be a simple aesthetic preference, or part of a ritual of grooming and control within a dynamic.


💬 Join the conversation: Is grooming part of your dynamic, such as a task, rule or ritual? Did you know there was a word for this? Share your own take.
=====
Word of the Week: Age Play
A form of role play built around age, most often Daddy/little or Mommy/little dynamics. It’s played only between consenting adults, and for many people it’s about nurturing, comfort, care and letting go of adult responsibilities, not about sex.


💬 Join the conversation: What do people most misunderstand about age play? If you’re a Caregiver or little, what does the dynamic give you? How would you define it?
=====
Word of the Week: Algolagnia
Experiencing pain as sexual pleasure, or turning pain into pleasure. It’s closely related to sadomasochism (SM). Algolagnia focuses on how pain itself becomes pleasurable.


💬 Join the conversation: What makes pain feel good for you, or what do you enjoy about giving it? Is there a difference between “good pain” and “bad pain”? Tell us your definition.
=====
Word of the Week: Bastinado
Striking the soles of the feet, usually with a cane, paddle or flogger. The bottom is often restrained so they can’t move away. It’s a form of impact play that can be intense because the feet are so sensitive. Go lightly and avoid the bones and joints.


💬 Join the conversation: Have you given or received bastinado? Which implement works best? Is it a favorite or a hard pass for you?
=====
Word of the Week: Body Fluid Monogamy (Fluid Bonding)
An agreement to share body fluids with only one partner as a safer-sex practice. Partners usually get tested first, for HIV and STIs and sometimes Hepatitis B and C. They may still play with others, but only with strict barrier protection.


💬 Join the conversation: How do you and your partner(s) talk about fluid bonding and testing? What does fluid bonding mean to you emotionally as well as practically? Share how you’d define it.
=====
Word of the Week: Boundaries
The limits you negotiate before play: what’s on the table, what’s off, and what needs care. Good boundaries are what make deep trust and intense play possible.


💬 Join the conversation: How do you communicate your boundaries? Have yours changed as you’ve grown in the lifestyle? What does “respecting boundaries” look like in practice?
=====
Word of the Week: Catharsis
The release of emotion or stress during or after a scene, whether intended or not. Tears, laughter and a deep sense of relief are all common, and sometimes the scene uncovers more than anyone expected.


💬 Join the conversation: Have you had a cathartic scene? Was it planned or a surprise? How do you take care of each other afterward? Give us your definition.
=====
Word of the Week: Clit Torture
Play that applies intense sensation to the clitoris and vulva for pleasure, through clamps, impact, suction, temperature or overstimulation. As with any play involving the genitals, communication and gradual build-up matter.


💬 Join the conversation: Is this a favorite, a soft limit or a hard limit for you? What’s the difference between “torture” and “tease”? How would you define it?
=====
Word of the Week: Cock and Ball Torture (CBT)
Applying intense sensation to the penis and testicles for pleasure, including squeezing, impact, binding, weights and more. Know the anatomy, start slowly, and stay away from anything that cuts off blood flow for too long.


💬 Join the conversation: What drew you to CBT, as a giver or receiver? Any safety tips for beginners? Share your own take.
=====
Word of the Week: Conditioning
A psychology term for deliberately linking a response to a stimulus. Much of what the lifestyle calls “training” or “slave training” uses classic conditioning techniques. Psych 101 would have huge enrollment if professors realized that training a sub to orgasm on command is far more interesting than training pigeons to play ping-pong!


💬 Join the conversation: Have you used conditioning in your dynamic, such as a word, a touch or a sound? What worked, and what surprised you? How do you define “training”?
=====
Word of the Week: Consensual Non-Consent (CNC)
An agreement, made in advance and within defined limits, that consent is given ahead of time without knowing exactly what’s planned. It takes a lot of trust and is usually only for partners who know each other well. Clear limits, clear safewords or signals, and thorough aftercare are essential.


💬 Join the conversation: What makes CNC feel safe enough to explore? How do you negotiate something meant to feel unnegotiated? Is consent ever truly “pre-given”? Share your definition.
=====
Word of the Week: Emotional Buttons (Emotional Triggers)
Words, behaviors or activities that set off a strong emotional reaction. Dominants should learn their submissive’s triggers, both the ones that bring out positive responses and the ones that cause harm. Knowing them is part of playing responsibly.


💬 Join the conversation: How do you find out your partner’s buttons before you accidentally press one? Have you ever hit an unexpected trigger in a scene? What happened next?
=====
Word of the Week: Fisting
Inserting the whole hand into the vagina (vaginal fisting) or anus (anal fisting, also called “handballing”). It takes patience, lots of lubricant, trimmed nails and gloves, and a slow, gradual build-up.


💬 Join the conversation: What’s the biggest myth about fisting? Any patience or prep tips? How would you describe it to a newcomer?
=====
Word of the Week: Flying (Subspace)
A transcendent state some people reach during a scene. It’s described so consistently by different people that it’s hard to doubt. Submissives describe something like an out-of-body experience, often with a strong connection to their Dominant: a tether, or the feeling of being wrapped in their protection. The elation and spaciness can last for hours or even days afterward.


💬 Join the conversation: Have you flown? How would you describe it? Dominants, do you experience your own version (“top space”)? How do you handle the drop afterward?
=====
Word of the Week: Humiliation
Playful embarrassment, such as teasing someone about their desires, as part of erotic control. Done well, it can actually build self-esteem by affirming someone’s sexuality. Real attacks on a person’s self-worth, or manipulation meant to break their spirit, are abuse, not SM.


💬 Join the conversation: Where’s the line between erotic humiliation and real harm? How do you negotiate it? Does humiliation play make you feel more powerful or more vulnerable?
=====
Word of the Week: Objectification
Consensually treating a submissive as an object, such as furniture, a decoration or a possession, for the pleasure of both partners. Many submissives find it freeing to set aside their identity for a while.


💬 Join the conversation: What’s appealing about being treated as an object, or treating someone as one? Have you been a footstool, a table or a piece of art? Tell us your definition.
=====
Word of the Week: Ponygirl / Ponyboy (Pony Play)
A classic fantasy made famous by the drawings of John Willie and Anne Rice’s Sleeping Beauty trilogy. Typical gear includes a horsehair tail on a butt plug, a bit gag or bridle head harness, and reins, often with high heels or hoof boots, a corset and plumes in the hair. The arms are usually bound behind the back. Activities range from being shown and led by the reins to being ridden or pulling a cart.


💬 Join the conversation: Any ponies or trainers out there? Is it about the gear, the training, the performance or the mindset? Show us your tack!
=====
Word of the Week: Power Exchange Levels
A system that matches depth of emotional involvement to degree of power exchange. The five levels are: 1) Conditional compliance, 2) Restricted ongoing acquiescence, 3) Provisional submission, 4) The covenant of dominance and submission, and 5) Absolute ownership. The first four are rooted in reality, while the fifth is mostly fantasy.


💬 Join the conversation: Which level best describes your dynamic? Has it shifted over time? Do you think Level 5 can exist in real life?
=====
Word of the Week: Sadomasomythology
Society’s misconceptions about what sadomasochism really involves, and also any poorly informed idea about a particular SM practice.


💬 Join the conversation: What misconceptions did you have about S&M before you joined the lifestyle? Which myth do you most wish would go away?
=====
Word of the Week: Slave
A submissive who consensually gives up total control over one or more areas of their life to another person, their Master or Mistress. It’s a controversial topic.


💬 Join the conversation: What do you think it means to be a slave? Can consent be withdrawn without destroying the dynamic? How is a slave different from a submissive?
=====
Word of the Week: Soft Limits
Activities someone is hesitant or nervous about but might try under the right conditions, ideally negotiated in gradually, starting at a trial or beginner level.


💬 Join the conversation: Have any of your soft limits stopped being limits? Even more interesting: have any of them turned into hard limits, and why?
=====
Word of the Week: Submissive Uniform
A style of dress worn by submissives that emphasizes their sexuality. It’s usually very short skirts, revealing tops, high heels, and stockings held up by a garter belt or bustier. Men, including crossdressers, have their own versions too.


💬 Join the conversation: Do you have a submissive uniform? What does it look like, and who chose it? Does putting it on change your headspace?
=====
Word of the Week: Vampire Gloves
Usually thin leather driving gloves with sharp metal tines or tacks lining the palms. The tines are typically snap or rhinestone settings pushed through the leather from inside and glued in place, though some very mean gloves use thumb or carpet tacks. Fur mitts can get the same treatment, giving textures from very nice to yowee! This is one of my favorite toys.


💬 Join the conversation: Anyone else out there use vampire gloves? Do you go soft and teasing or full yowee? DIY or store-bought?
=====
Word of the Week: Owner / Property
A dynamic in which one partner “owns” the other, usually with explicit agreements about expectations, responsibilities and care. Ownership involves responsibility as well as control.


💬 Join the conversation: What does ownership mean in your dynamic? Collars, contracts, rituals? What responsibilities does an Owner take on? Share your definition.
=====
Word of the Week: Butler / Maid
A service role in which the submissive serves in a domestic capacity: cleaning, cooking, serving drinks, managing the household and attending to their Dominant’s comfort.


💬 Join the conversation: Is anyone here in a service dynamic based on butler/maid roles? What does a day of service look like? Is it about the uniform, the tasks or the devotion?
=====
Word of the Week: Exhibitionist
A person who enjoys being on display in public or semi-public settings to attract attention. In the lifestyle, consent applies to the audience too, so the right venue makes all the difference.


💬 Join the conversation: Are you an exhibitionist? What draws you to putting yourself on display: the attention, the vulnerability or the thrill?
=====
Word of the Week: Voyeur
A person who enjoys watching others, usually in a sexual context, without joining in. At a play party, watching respectfully is an art in itself: keep a respectful distance, stay quiet, and never interrupt a scene.


💬 Join the conversation: Voyeurs, are there particular conditions that make your interest peak? What’s good play-party etiquette for watchers?
=====
Word of the Week: Swinger
A person who has consensual sex with others outside their committed relationship, usually as a couple. I’ve always felt swingers belong under the kink umbrella.


💬 Join the conversation: What do you think? Are swingers kinky or not? Where do the swinger and BDSM communities overlap, and where do they differ?
=====
Word of the Week: Doll (Doll Play)
A person who enjoys objectification and wants to become a living doll, transformed by their own efforts, someone else’s, or both. Transformation can involve makeup, wigs, costumes, masks, posing and stillness. I’ve seen couples go to extraordinary lengths to set up transformation scenes.


💬 Join the conversation: This one’s fascinating. Any dolls or doll owners care to share? What does the transformation give you?
=====
Word of the Week: Wax Play
The top drips hot wax onto the bottom’s skin for sensation, visual drama, or both. Use low-temperature candles such as soy or paraffin made for play, never beeswax, and test the heat on yourself first. The higher you drip from, the cooler the wax is when it lands.


💬 Join the conversation: Wax play always looks so interesting, and it seems like it could be very relaxing. But I never seem to do it because it’s so messy! Any tips for keeping the cleanup manageable? Favorite candles?
=====
Word of the Week: Aftercare
The care partners give each other after a scene, such as blankets, water, snacks, cuddles, reassurance, or quiet time. Intense play can leave both people emotionally and physically raw, and aftercare helps everyone come back down gently. Tops need it too!


💬 Join the conversation: What does your ideal aftercare look like? Has your aftercare changed over time? Tops, what do you need after a scene?
=====
Word of the Week: Safeword
A word or signal agreed in advance that stops or slows a scene immediately. Many people use the traffic-light system: Green (keep going), Yellow (slow down or check in), Red (stop). If speaking isn’t possible, use a nonverbal signal such as dropping a ball or tapping out.


💬 Join the conversation: What’s your safeword? Have you ever used it, or had someone use it with you? How did it feel? What makes a good safeword?
=====
Word of the Week: Sub Drop
The emotional and physical crash that can follow intense play, sometimes hours or days later. It can bring sadness, anxiety, exhaustion or a feeling of emptiness as the body comes down from its high. Good aftercare and checking in the next day both help.


💬 Join the conversation: Have you experienced sub drop? What helps you through it? Dominants, have you felt “Dom drop”?
=====
Word of the Week: Negotiation
The conversation before play in which partners agree on limits, desires, safewords, health concerns and aftercare. Good negotiation doesn’t spoil the mood. It’s the foundation that lets everyone relax and fully let go.


💬 Join the conversation: How formal are your negotiations, from checklists to casual chats? What’s one question everyone should ask before playing with someone new?
=====
Word of the Week: SSC (Safe, Sane, Consensual)
One of the founding principles of BDSM: play should be Safe (risks are minimized), Sane (everyone is clear-headed and can tell fantasy from reality), and Consensual (everyone involved agrees).


💬 Join the conversation: Is SSC still the right standard? Can any play be truly “safe”? How do you put these principles into practice?
=====
Word of the Week: RACK (Risk-Aware Consensual Kink)
An alternative to SSC. It recognizes that no kink is completely risk-free and asks that everyone understand the risks and consent to them. It emphasizes informed choice over an idea of perfect safety.


💬 Join the conversation: Do you follow SSC, RACK, or something else? How do you weigh the risks of edgier play?
=====
Word of the Week: Switch
Someone who enjoys both the dominant and submissive roles, depending on the partner, mood or scene. Some switch within one relationship, while others are Dominant with one partner and submissive with another.


💬 Join the conversation: Any switches out there? What decides which side comes out? Do people underestimate switches?
=====
Word of the Week: Brat
A submissive who playfully pushes back, teases and disobeys to provoke their Dominant into taking control. Bratting is a game: the defiance is part of the fun, not real disrespect.


💬 Join the conversation: Brats, what’s your favorite way to cause trouble? Is bratting submission or resistance? Where’s the line between bratty and rude?
=====
Word of the Week: Brat Tamer
A Dominant who enjoys the challenge of a brat, matching their mischief with wit, patience and firm, playful consequences. It’s a dance of wills that both partners enjoy.


💬 Join the conversation: Brat Tamers, what’s your secret? What’s the most creative “taming” you’ve done? Is it about winning or about the chase?
=====
Word of the Week: Collaring
Giving a collar as a symbol of commitment, ownership or belonging within a dynamic. Collars range from training or “consideration” collars to formal collaring ceremonies, which some people treat as seriously as a wedding. Many people wear discreet “day collars,” such as necklaces or bracelets, in everyday life.


💬 Join the conversation: What does a collar mean to you? Have you had a collaring ceremony? Show us your day collar!
=====
Word of the Week: Protocol
The rules and rituals that shape a dynamic, such as forms of address, posture, how to ask permission, and how to serve. Protocols can be high (formal and strict) or low (relaxed and everyday) and can change depending on the setting.


💬 Join the conversation: What protocols do you follow? Do you use different levels in public, at home and at parties? What’s your favorite ritual?
=====
Word of the Week: Impact Play
Striking the body for sensation, with a hand, paddle, flogger, crop, cane or strap. Sensations range from thuddy (deep, heavy) to stingy (sharp, surface-level). Stick to fleshy areas like the buttocks and thighs, and avoid the kidneys, spine and joints.


💬 Join the conversation: Thud or sting? What’s your favorite implement? What’s the most important impact safety tip?
=====
Word of the Week: Shibari (Kinbaku)
A Japanese style of rope bondage known for its beauty, patterns and the emotional connection between the person tying and the person tied. It can be artistic, meditative, erotic, or all three. Learn nerve safety and always keep safety shears within reach.


💬 Join the conversation: What draws you to rope: the look, the restraint or the connection? Where did you learn to tie? Favorite tie?
=====
Word of the Week: Rope Bunny
Someone who loves being tied up. For many rope bunnies, the experience is about surrender, trust, the feel of the rope and the meditative “rope space” it can create.


💬 Join the conversation: Rope bunnies, what does being tied give you? Favorite rope: jute, hemp or cotton? Are you a bunny who also ties?
=====
Word of the Week: Primal Play
Play driven by instinct rather than structure. Partners let their animal side out through chasing, wrestling, growling, biting and claiming. It’s raw, physical and often wordless, so negotiate limits well beforehand.


💬 Join the conversation: Predator or prey? What draws you to primal play? How is it different from structured D/s?
=====
Word of the Week: Pet Play
Role play in which someone takes on the persona of an animal, such as a puppy, kitten or pony, usually with a handler or owner. Gear often includes ears, tails, collars, leashes and mitts. For many people it’s about joyful, uncomplicated headspace rather than sex.


💬 Join the conversation: Any pups or kittens here? What’s your pet persona like? Handlers, what do you enjoy about caring for a pet?
=====
Word of the Week: Edging
Bringing someone close to orgasm again and again, stopping just before they get there. It builds intense arousal and, eventually, a more powerful release, if release is allowed at all.


💬 Join the conversation: How long is your record? Is edging pleasure or torture? Edger or edgee?
=====
Word of the Week: Orgasm Control
A dynamic in which one partner decides when, how, or whether the other is allowed to orgasm. It includes orgasm denial, forced orgasms and orgasm on command, and can last for one scene or a long-term arrangement.


💬 Join the conversation: Have you given up control of your orgasms? What’s harder: denial or orgasm on command? How does it change your dynamic?
=====
Word of the Week: Sensory Deprivation
Removing one or more senses with blindfolds, earplugs, hoods or restraints to heighten the others and deepen surrender. Without sight or sound, every touch feels more intense. Stay close and keep checking in.


💬 Join the conversation: Which sense is hardest for you to give up? Does sensory deprivation relax you or make you anxious? Favorite gear?
=====
Word of the Week: Predicament Bondage
Bondage set up so that every escape from one discomfort causes another. For example, relaxing tired legs pulls on a sensitive spot. It’s a mental puzzle as much as a physical one. Never leave someone alone in it.


💬 Join the conversation: What’s the most devious predicament you’ve set up or been in? What makes a predicament fun rather than miserable?
=====
Word of the Week: Dungeon Monitor (DM)
A volunteer who watches over a play space, making sure scenes are safe, rules are followed and help is available when needed. DMs are the unsung heroes of every good play party.


💬 Join the conversation: Have you been a DM? What should every DM know? Have you seen a DM handle a situation well?
=====
Word of the Week: Munch
A casual social meetup for kinky people, usually at a restaurant or café, with no play and no dress code. Munches are a low-pressure way for newcomers to meet the community and ask questions.


💬 Join the conversation: Do you remember your first munch? Any tips for nervous newcomers? What makes a munch welcoming?
=====
Word of the Week: Scene
A negotiated period of play with a beginning and an end. “The scene” can also mean the BDSM community as a whole, as in “How long have you been in the scene?”


💬 Join the conversation: What makes a scene memorable for you? Do you plan scenes in detail or let them unfold? How long have you been in the scene?
=====
Word of the Week: Top / Bottom
The Top is the person doing the action in a scene, such as tying, spanking or teasing. The Bottom is the person receiving it. These are roles within a scene and are separate from Dominant/submissive identity. A submissive can top, and a Dominant can bottom.


💬 Join the conversation: How do you explain the difference between Top/Bottom and Dom/sub? Have you topped as a sub or bottomed as a Dom?
=====
Word of the Week: Topping from the Bottom
When a bottom tries to steer a scene from below by giving directions, making demands, or quietly manipulating the action. Some see it as disrespectful, while others see it as clear communication. Context matters!


💬 Join the conversation: Where’s the line between topping from the bottom and good communication? Have you been guilty of it? How do you handle it as a Top?
=====
Word of the Week: Service Submission
A form of submission focused on acts of service, such as cooking, cleaning, running errands, giving massages or anticipating needs. For service submissives, being useful is deeply satisfying and is their way of expressing devotion.


💬 Join the conversation: Service subs, what’s your favorite way to serve? Dominants, how do you show appreciation for service? Is service always sexual?
=====
Word of the Week: Funishment
A playful “punishment” that both partners actually enjoy. It’s often a game, especially with brats. Real punishment is meant to correct behavior, while funishment is mostly an excuse to play.


💬 Join the conversation: Funishment or real punishment: which plays a bigger role in your dynamic? What’s your favorite funishment? Can real punishment be healthy?
=====
Word of the Week: Mindfuck
Psychological play that messes with someone’s perception, expectations or sense of reality. For example, a Dominant convinces a blindfolded submissive that an ice cube is a hot brand. It’s powerful and takes a lot of trust, along with careful negotiation and aftercare.


💬 Join the conversation: What’s the best mindfuck you’ve given or received? Where’s the line between a fun mindfuck and a harmful one?
=====
Word of the Week: Hard Limits
Activities that are completely off the table, with no negotiation and no exceptions. Hard limits must always be respected, and crossing one is a serious breach of trust.


💬 Join the conversation: How did you discover your hard limits? Have any hard limits changed over time? How do you make sure a partner truly respects them?
=====
Word of the Week: Violet Wand
A vintage electrical device that produces a high-voltage, low-current charge, creating sparks, tingles and crackling sensations on the skin. The glass electrodes glow purple, which makes for spectacular visuals in a dark room. Keep it away from flammable products and avoid using it above the waist if anyone has heart problems.


💬 Join the conversation: Have you played with a violet wand? Tingle or zap? Favorite electrode? What other electro toys do you use?
=====
Word of the Week: Vanilla
Non-kinky, conventional sex or people. Vanilla isn’t an insult, and plenty of kinksters enjoy vanilla sex too. The question of how “vanilla” and “kink” relate is endlessly interesting.


💬 Join the conversation: How do you explain kink to vanilla friends? Do you have a vanilla side? Is vanilla really that plain?
=====
